  "Everything in Ekbasis-27B's limits applies, including confident errors in the world families it was trained on.",
  "More confident errors in unfamiliar worlds: on the 5,364 items of never-trained world families of the published confident-errors set, Skopos is wrong slightly less often than Ekbasis-27B (14.26% against 14.93%), but more often with confidence 0.9 or more: 4.49 against 3.75 wrong answers per 100 (+0.75 [+0.28, +1.23]; on the answers the actions change +1.19 [+0.52, +1.89]). In unfamiliar domains, let it look when unsure (simulate), read calibrated rather than the raw confidence, and act on a confident answer only at a stricter threshold than in familiar ones.",
  "In-distribution and selected gains: the largest gains (executed factory scenarios, real git sequences, agent sessions) are on sets built by the same generators as its training rows, and three of them (fac_val, git_long, git_long_val) are the sets whose pre-registered rules chose this version over its siblings (R1 → R2 → R4): selection adds to the in-distribution advantage. The independent evidence is smaller: the fresh never-trained families (+1.6 points) and the neutral agent turns (AUROC level with Ekbasis-27B).",
  "\"Held-out\" git command types: the release protocol's held-out types (git rebase <branch> and git restore --source=HEAD~1 <file>) are held out of the git sandbox's training rows, but Skopos' shell and git factory rows contain both, in other repositories (the overlap audit's note): read the held-out rows as a weaker test for Skopos than for Ekbasis-27B.",
  "Long chains in written-rule worlds: answering in one pass, its accuracy falls with the number of actions (87.9% over 1–20 actions on 828 items, the overlap-free part of fhorizon, from the generator of its plan-level training rows: in-distribution); a model that simulates step by step does better there at far higher cost. Use simulate (look when unsure) for long chains.",
  "Images: the state can be text or an image, as in Ekbasis-27B. This version's last training was text only; on 395 fresh image items (lamps and containers; INT4 against INT4, served), its image ability is preserved by the pre-registered criterion (PREREG_IMAGE.md: at most 2 points lower): 79.8% against 78.7% when the state is an image (+1.0 [−2.0, +4.1]), and reading the final state from a picture 94.2% against 91.9% (+2.3 [+0.5, +4.3]). Predicting from a picture stays weaker than from text (91.9% on the same items)."
